Cognitive Field Computing: Alignment-Constrained Fractional Dynamics on Hybrid Information Manifolds

A Corrected and Verified Mathematical Foundation

---

Abstract

We present a corrected mathematical framework for Cognitive Field Computing (CFC). The central object is a non-negative density field $\Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta,t)$ on a product domain $\Omega \times \Theta$, evolving under a time-fractional reaction–diffusion equation with Caputo derivative of order $\alpha \in (0,1)$. Alignment is enforced by a smoothed projection operator acting on the field's normalized belief distribution at each spatial point.

This revision corrects four classes of error in earlier formulations and eliminates the central theoretical gap:

1. Fractional operator status. The Mittag-Leffler family $E_\alpha(t^\alpha A)$ is demoted from a semigroup to a solution operator family; the invalid fractional chain rule is replaced by the fractional comparison principle.
2. Thermodynamic consistency. Entropy production is derived from the geometric decomposition of Nagayama et al., with an explicit gradient-structure hypothesis guaranteeing non-negativity. A sign error in the reaction term is corrected.
3. Information geometry. A common manifold is constructed for the Fisher–Rao and Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao metrics, resolving the dimensional mismatch.
4. Compositional alignment. The Kleisli composition estimate is corrected using convexity of the Kullback–Leibler divergence.

Key advance in this revision. The previously conjectural sign of the fractional Leibniz remainder (Conjecture 3.10) is now established as Lemma 3.10 via the fractional Lyapunov inequality of Gomoyunov (arXiv:1710.07003) and its generalizations by Agarwal–Hristova–O'Regan. Consequently, Hypothesis (H-AI) is eliminated, and Proposition 3.6 (Alignment Invariance), Theorem 10.3 (Coherence Preservation), and Theorem 11.2 (Coupled System Existence) are upgraded from conditional to unconditional theorems.

We close with a status table classifying every claim as theorem, conditional theorem, or conjecture.

Keywords: fractional reaction–diffusion, Caputo derivative, maximal regularity, alignment, mean-field games, information geometry, categorical compositionality, thermodynamic stability, geometric coherence.

