0. Assumptions and Notation

Domain. $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^d$ is a bounded open set with smooth boundary $\partial\Omega$. $\Theta$ is a Polish space (typically a compact subset of $\mathbb{R}^m$) equipped with a reference measure $\mu_\Theta$.

Time horizon. $T \in (0,\infty)$ is fixed; all statements are for $t \in [0,T]$ unless stated otherwise.

Fractional order. $\alpha \in (0,1)$ is fixed.

Function spaces.

· $H = L^2(\Omega \times \Theta; \mu_\Omega \otimes \mu_\Theta)$, with inner product $\langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle$.
· $H^1 = H^1(\Omega \times \Theta)$ with norm $\|f\|_{H^1}^2 = \|f\|^2 + \|\nabla f\|^2$.
· $H^2_N$ denotes $H^2(\Omega \times \Theta)$ with Neumann boundary conditions.
· $C([0,T]; X)$ denotes continuous $X$-valued functions; $L^p(0,T;X)$ is the usual Bochner space.
· $\mathcal{P}(\Theta)$ denotes Borel probability measures on $\Theta$.

Field. $\Psi: \Omega \times \Theta \times [0,T] \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ is the intelligence density field. We write $\Psi(x,\theta,t)$ or $\Psi(t)$ when arguments are suppressed. The normalized belief distribution at $(x,t)$ is
p_{x,t}(\theta) = \frac{\Psi(x,\theta,t)}{\rho(x,t)}, \qquad \rho(x,t) := \int_\Theta \Psi(x,\theta,t)\,d\mu_\Theta(\theta),


defined whenever $\rho(x,t) > 0$.

Target distribution. $\mathcal{V} \in \mathcal{P}(\Theta)$ is a fixed reference (value) distribution, absolutely continuous with respect to $\mu_\Theta$ with density also denoted $\mathcal{V}$.

Standing assumption (SA1). $\Psi_0 \in H^1 \cap L^\infty$ with $\Psi_0 \ge 0$ and $\rho_0(x) := \int_\Theta \Psi_0(x,\theta)\,d\mu_\Theta(\theta) \ge \delta_{\min} > 0$.

Standing assumption (SA2). $\mathcal{V}$ has a positive, continuous density with $\mathcal{V}(\theta) \ge \nu_{\min} > 0$ on $\Theta$.

Standing assumption (SA3). The diffusion coefficient $D$ depends on $\Psi$ through a bounded, Lipschitz observable $\sigma \in L^\infty(\Omega \times \Theta \times [0,T])$. This ensures that $A(\Psi) = -\nabla \cdot (D(\sigma) \nabla \cdot)$ depends Lipschitz-continuously on $\Psi$ in the interpolation space $H^1$, as required by Theorem 2.4.

Standing assumption (SA4). The Gomoyunov–Agarwal–Hristova–O'Regan fractional Lyapunov inequality (Lemma 2.6 below) applies to the alignment functional $\mathcal{A}$ under (SA1)–(SA2). This is verified in Lemma 3.10.

Where the strict positivity of $\Psi$ is required, we work with a regularized divergence (Remark 3.2).

