1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The dominant paradigm of artificial intelligence treats cognition as discrete computation. Biological intelligence is continuous, non-Markovian, and spatially distributed. CFC models intelligence as a density field on a product of a physical (or information) manifold and a belief space.

1.2 Alignment as a Gating Constraint

Recent AI-safety literature treats alignment as a gating factor for scaling. CFC therefore embeds alignment into the field equation as a projection onto a sublevel set of a divergence functional, rather than treating it as a downstream engineering problem.

1.3 Scope of This Revision

The previous iteration of this framework contained four classes of mathematical defect: (i) an erroneous appeal to the semigroup property of Mittag-Leffler operators and an invalid fractional chain rule; (ii) an entropy production formula that could become negative; (iii) a hybrid metric combining two geometrically incompatible structures; (iv) a Kleisli composition estimate resting on a non-standard chain rule. This revision corrects each defect at its source and states every result with explicit hypotheses.

This revision additionally resolves the central open problem of the prior version. The sign of the fractional Leibniz remainder (Conjecture 3.10 in the prior revision) is established by Lemma 3.10 below, using the fractional Lyapunov inequality of Gomoyunov and its generalization by Agarwal–Hristova–O'Regan. As a consequence, Hypothesis (H-AI) is eliminated, and the alignment invariance, coherence preservation, and coupled-system existence results become unconditional.

1.4 Contributions

1. Fractional Lyapunov inequality replaces the invalid chain rule (Lemma 2.6), rendering alignment invariance unconditional (Proposition 3.6, Lemma 3.10).
2. Maximal regularity for quasilinear fractional problems (Clément–Londen–Simonett, Zacher) provides local and global well-posedness (§4).
3. Gradient structure on the reaction term yields non-negative entropy production via the Nagayama et al. decomposition, with the sign convention of Proposition 5.2 corrected (§5).
4. Common manifold constructed for the hybrid Fisher–WFR metric (§7).
5. Convexity of KL divergence yields a strictly stronger Kleisli composition bound (§8).
6. Reputation-aware mean-field game provides adversarial robustness (§9).
7. Geometric coherence formalized via spectral gap, Ricci curvature, and basin stability (§10).
8. Time-fractional coupling enforced in the full system, consistent with Camilli–De Maio well-posedness (§11).

