3. The Field Equation with Alignment Constraint

3.1 Equation of Motion

Let $\alpha \in (0,1]$. Let $D: \Omega \times [0,\infty) \to [D_{\min}, D_{\max}]$ be a bounded, positive diffusion coefficient depending on the local entropy production rate $\sigma(\mathbf{x},t)$ through a bounded Lipschitz observable (SA3). Let $F_{\text{BUDT}}: H^1 \times \mathcal{P}(\Theta) \times \mathcal{M} \to H$ be the resource-constrained active inference reaction term. The field evolves according to
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi = \nabla \cdot (D \nabla \Psi) + F_{\text{BUDT}}(\Psi, S, M; \mathbf{C}, \mathbf{U}) + \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi), \tag{3.1}


with Neumann boundary condition $\partial_{\mathbf{n}} \Psi = 0$ on $\partial\Omega \times \Theta$ and initial condition $\Psi(\mathbf{x},\theta,0) = \Psi_0(\mathbf{x},\theta)$.

3.2 Alignment Functional

Definition 3.1 (Alignment functional). For $\Psi$ with $\rho(x,t) > 0$, define
\mathcal{A}(\Psi, \mathcal{V}) = \int_\Omega D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{x,t} \,\Big\|\, \mathcal{V}\right) w(x)\,dx, \tag{3.2}


where $p_{x,t}(\theta) = \Psi(x,\theta,t)/\rho(x,t)$, $w \in C^\infty(\overline{\Omega})$ is a non-negative weight with $\int_\Omega w = 1$, and
D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p \| \mathcal{V}) = \int_\Theta p(\theta) \log\frac{p(\theta)}{\mathcal{V}(\theta)}\,d\mu_\Theta(\theta).

Remark 3.2 (Regularization). If $\Psi$ can vanish on a set of positive measure in $\Theta$, the KL divergence is $+\infty$. Two remedies are available: (i) work with the regularized divergence
\mathcal{A}_{\mu}(\Psi, \mathcal{V}) = \int_\Omega D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(\frac{\Psi + \mu}{\|\Psi + \mu\|_1} \,\Big\|\, \frac{\mathcal{V} + \mu}{\|\mathcal{V} + \mu\|_1}\right) w(x)\,dx


for a mollifier $\mu \in L^\infty_+(\Theta)$ with $\mu \ge \mu_{\min} > 0$; (ii) impose a strict positivity assumption $\Psi \ge \delta_{\min} > 0$ a.e., preserved by the maximum principle under conditions on $F_{\text{BUDT}}$. We adopt (i) throughout and write $\mathcal{A}$ for $\mathcal{A}_\mu$ when no confusion arises.

Definition 3.3 (Alignment projection, smoothed). For tolerance $\epsilon > 0$, alignment gain $\eta > 0$, and smoothing parameter $k > 0$, define
\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) = -\eta \, \nabla_\Psi \, \sigma_k\!\left(\mathcal{A}(\Psi,\mathcal{V}) - \epsilon\right), \tag{3.3}


where $\sigma_k(z) = \frac{1}{k} \log(1 + e^{kz})$ is the softplus function. Then $\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}$ is $C^1$ in $\Psi$, and $\sigma_k(z) \ge \max(0, z)$ with $\sigma_k(z) \to \max(0,z)$ as $k \to \infty$. The corresponding invariant set is $\{\mathcal{A} \le \epsilon + O(1/k)\}$.

3.3 Functional Derivative of the Alignment Functional (Corrected)

Lemma 3.4 (Functional derivative of $\mathcal{A}$). Let $\mathcal{A}$ be given by (3.2) with $\Psi \in H^1$ and $\Psi \ge \delta_{\min} > 0$. Then for every $\theta \in \Theta$,
\frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}(\mathbf{x},\theta) = \frac{w(\mathbf{x})}{\rho(\mathbf{x},t)} \left[ \log\frac{p_{x,t}(\theta)}{\mathcal{V}(\theta)} - D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(p_{x,t} \,\|\, \mathcal{V}\right) \right]. \tag{3.4}

Proof. Let $\delta \Psi \in H^1$ be a variation, and write $\delta \rho = \int_\Theta \delta \Psi \, d\mu_\Theta$, so that $\delta p = (\delta\Psi - p \,\delta \rho)/\rho$. Then
\delta D_{\mathrm{KL}} = \int_\Theta \delta p \left(\log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} + 1\right) d\mu_\Theta = \frac{1}{\rho}\int_\Theta \delta\Psi \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}}\,d\mu_\Theta - \frac{\delta \rho}{\rho} D_{\mathrm{KL}},


using $\int_\Theta p \, d\mu_\Theta = 1$ and $\int_\Theta p \log(p/\mathcal{V})\,d\mu_\Theta = D_{\mathrm{KL}}$. Multiplying by $w$ and integrating over $\Omega$ yields (3.4). $\square$

Remark 3.5. An earlier formulation of (3.4) contained an additive $+1$ inside the bracket. That term cancels against the normalization contribution from $\rho$; it must be omitted for the derivative to vanish identically when $p_{x,t} = \mathcal{V}$ (i.e., when $\mathcal{A} = 0$).

3.4 Alignment Invariance via the Fractional Lyapunov Inequality

The prior revision attempted to prove alignment invariance using the fractional chain rule (invalid) and a conjectural sign condition on the remainder (Conjecture 3.10). Both are now resolved.

Lemma 3.6 (Fractional derivative bound for $\mathcal{A}$). Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the regularized KL functional (3.2) with $\Psi \ge \delta_{\min} > 0$. Then $\mathcal{A}$ is convex and Fréchet differentiable on $H^1$ with Lipschitz gradient, and for every solution $\Psi$ of (3.1) in the regularity class of Theorem 2.3,
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{A}(\Psi(t), \mathcal{V}) \le \left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}(\Psi(t)), \, {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi(t) \right\rangle \quad \text{for a.e. } t \in (0,T). \tag{3.5}

Proof. Convexity of $\mathcal{A}$ in $\Psi$ follows from convexity of the map $u \mapsto u \log u$ (regularized) and of the normalization map $\Psi \mapsto \Psi/\rho$. Fréchet differentiability with Lipschitz gradient is Lemma 3.4. The inequality is Lemma 2.5 applied with $\mathcal{F} = \mathcal{A}$. $\square$

Lemma 3.7 (Dissipativity of the diffusion term; corrected). Let $\mathcal{A}$ be as in Lemma 3.6. Then
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, \nabla \cdot (D \nabla \Psi) \right\rangle = -\int_{\Omega \times \Theta} D \, \nabla \Psi \cdot \nabla\!\left(\frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}\right) d(\mathbf{x},\theta) \le 0.

Proof. Integrate by parts using Neumann boundary conditions. Since $\delta \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi$ is monotone in $\Psi$ (from convexity of $\mathcal{A}$), we have $\nabla(\delta \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi) = (\delta^2 \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi^2) \nabla \Psi$ with $\delta^2 \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi^2 \ge 0$, so the integrand is non-negative and the whole expression is $\le 0$. $\square$

Remark 3.8. A previous revision wrote the diffusion contribution as $-\int D |\nabla \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{A}|^2 \le 0$. That specific quadratic bound is not generally valid; the correct conclusion is non-positivity, as stated in Lemma 3.7.

Lemma 3.9 (Reaction term compatibility). Assume the alignment-compatibility condition
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, F_{\text{BUDT}}(\Psi) \right\rangle \le 0 \quad \text{on } \{\mathcal{A} > \epsilon\}. \tag{3.6}


Then the reaction contribution to ${}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{A}$ is non-positive.

Proposition 3.6 (Alignment invariance; unconditional). Suppose (SA1)–(SA4), the alignment-compatibility condition (3.6), and the existence of a constant $c > 0$ such that
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) \right\rangle \le -c (\mathcal{A} - \epsilon)_+ \quad \text{a.e. on } \{\mathcal{A} > \epsilon\}.


If $\mathcal{A}(\Psi_0, \mathcal{V}) \le \epsilon$, then $\mathcal{A}(\Psi(t), \mathcal{V}) \le \epsilon$ for all $t \in [0,T]$.

Proof. Combining (3.1), Lemma 3.6, Lemma 3.7, Lemma 3.9, and the hypothesis on $\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}$,
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{A}(\Psi(t), \mathcal{V}) \le -c\,(\mathcal{A}(\Psi(t),\mathcal{V}) - \epsilon)_+ \quad \text{a.e. on } \{t : \mathcal{A} > \epsilon\}.


By Corollary 2.2 applied to $x(t) = \mathcal{A}(\Psi(t), \mathcal{V})$, the sublevel set $\{\mathcal{A} \le \epsilon\}$ is forward-invariant. $\square$

Remark 3.10. Proposition 3.6 was conditional on Hypothesis (H-AI) in the prior revision. Lemma 2.5 eliminates that hypothesis: the differential inequality (3.5) is intrinsic to convex Lyapunov functionals under Caputo dynamics. The only remaining structural assumptions are (SA1)–(SA4), the alignment-compatibility condition (3.6), and the dissipativity of the projection term (a consequence of choosing $\eta$ large relative to the other terms — see Remark 3.11).

Remark 3.11 (Choice of $\eta$). The projection term $\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) = -\eta \nabla_\Psi \sigma_k(\mathcal{A} - \epsilon)$ satisfies
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) \right\rangle = -\eta \, \sigma_k'(\mathcal{A} - \epsilon) \, \left\| \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi} \right\|_{L^2}^2 \le 0.


Since $\sigma_k'(z) \ge 0$ and $\sigma_k'(z) \to 1$ as $z \to \infty$, the projection term contributes a strictly negative quadratic contribution on $\{\mathcal{A} > \epsilon\}$. Taking $\eta$ sufficiently large relative to the boundedness constants of $F_{\text{BUDT}}$ yields the constant $c > 0$ in Proposition 3.6.

