4. Well-Posedness of the Field Equation

4.1 Local Existence via Maximal Regularity

Theorem 4.1 (Local existence). Let (SA1)–(SA3) hold. Suppose:

· $F_{\text{BUDT}}: H^1 \to H$ is Lipschitz on bounded sets;
· $\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}: H^1 \to H$ is Lipschitz on bounded sets (this holds for the smoothed projection);
· $D$ satisfies (SA3).

Then there exist $T_0 > 0$ and a unique solution
\Psi \in C([0,T_0]; H^1) \cap C((0,T_0]; H^2_N), \qquad {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi \in C((0,T_0]; L^2),


of (3.1) with $\Psi(0) = \Psi_0$.

Proof. Apply Theorem 2.3 with $X_0 = L^2$, $X_1 = H^2_N$, $X_\gamma = H^1$. Under (SA3), $A(\Psi) = -\nabla\cdot(D(\sigma)\nabla\cdot)$ is sectorial with uniform constants $D_{\min} \le D \le D_{\max}$ and Lipschitz in $\Psi$ on bounded subsets of $H^1$. The nonlinearities $F_{\text{BUDT}}$ and $\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}$ are Lipschitz on bounded subsets of $H^1$, hence admissible. Maximal regularity yields existence, uniqueness, and regularity. $\square$

4.2 A Priori Bounds

Lemma 4.2 ($L^2$ bound). If $F_{\text{BUDT}}$ is dissipative in the sense $\langle \Psi, F_{\text{BUDT}}(\Psi) \rangle \le C_1 \|\Psi\|^2 + C_2$ and the projection term satisfies $\langle \Psi, \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) \rangle \le C_3$, then
\|\Psi(t)\|_{L^2}^2 \le \|\Psi_0\|_{L^2}^2 + C(T)


for a constant $C(T)$ depending on $T$ and the data.

Proof. Standard energy estimate applied to the mild formulation, using the boundedness of the solution operator and the fractional Grönwall inequality. $\square$

Lemma 4.3 ($H^1$ bound). Under the additional assumption $\langle \nabla \Psi, \nabla F_{\text{BUDT}}(\Psi) \rangle \le C_4 \|\nabla \Psi\|^2 + C_5$, the $H^1$ norm satisfies a similar bound.

4.3 Global Existence

Theorem 4.5 (Global existence). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.1 and Lemmas 4.2–4.3, the local solution extends to $[0,T]$ for every $T < \infty$.

Proof. The solution can be extended as long as it remains bounded in $H^1$. Lemmas 4.2–4.3 provide the required bound. Apply the continuation principle of Theorem 2.3. $\square$

4.4 Diffusion Modulation with Floor

Definition 4.6 (Adaptive diffusion). Given a bounded Lipschitz observable $\sigma(\mathbf{x},t)$ and headroom parameter $\sigma_{\max} > 0$, define
D(\mathbf{x},t) = \max\!\left\{ D_{\min}, \, D_0 \left(1 - \frac{\sigma(\mathbf{x},t)}{\sigma_{\max}}\right) \right\}. \tag{4.1}


This ensures $D_{\min} \le D \le D_0$, preserving uniform ellipticity.

Lemma 4.7 (Bounded entropy production). Under (4.1) and the entropy decomposition of §5, the total entropy production rate satisfies $\dot{S}_{\text{field}}(t) \le C$ for a constant $C$ depending on $D_0$, $\sigma_{\max}$, and the data.

Proof. The diffusion contribution is bounded above by $D_0 \|\nabla \Psi\|^2/T$. The reaction contribution is bounded by the gradient structure (see §5.2). The projection contribution is dissipative. Combine. $\square$

