6.2 Alignment-Enriched BUDT Operator

The resource-constrained optimal action is
\mathcal{B}[\Psi](\mathbf{x},t) = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \left\{ \mathcal{I}(a; \Psi) - \beta\, \mathrm{Tr}(\mathbf{C}^{-1}(\mathbf{x},t) \mathbf{U}(\mathbf{x},t;a)) - \gamma\, \mathcal{A}(\Psi_a, \mathcal{V}) \right\}, \tag{6.1}


where the last term penalizes actions that increase alignment divergence.

Proposition 6.1 (Lipschitz continuity). Under the regularity assumptions of the original BUDT operator (concave information gain, affine uncertainty, convex feasible set) and the convexity of $\mathcal{A}$ in the action, the map $\Psi \mapsto \mathcal{B}[\Psi]$ is Lipschitz from $L^2$ to $L^2(\Omega \times \Theta; \mathcal{A})$.

Proof. The objective is concave in $a$ (sum of a concave information term, a linear uncertainty term, and a concave alignment penalty). The argmax is unique and depends Lipschitz-continuously on $\Psi$ by the implicit function theorem. $\square$

