7. Information Geometry of the Cognitive Manifold

7.1 Common Manifold Construction

The Fisher–Rao metric $g^{\text{Fisher}}$ is a Riemannian metric on a statistical manifold. The Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao (WFR) metric $g^{\text{WFR}}$ is a Riemannian metric on a space of positive measures with unbalanced transport. These are not automatically defined on the same manifold, and the sum $g^{\text{hyb}} = (1-\lambda) g^{\text{Fisher}} + \lambda g^{\text{WFR}}$ requires a common tangent space.

Construction 7.1 (Common manifold). Let $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{P}(\Theta)$ be a parameterized family of densities $\{p_\theta : \theta \in \Theta_{\mathcal{M}}\}$ with $\Theta_{\mathcal{M}} \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ open, such that:

· $p_\theta$ is $C^1$ in $\theta$ and positive on $\Theta$;
· The Fisher information matrix $g^{\text{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta) = \int_\Theta \partial_i \log p_\theta \, \partial_j \log p_\theta \, p_\theta \, d\mu_\Theta$ is well-defined (possibly degenerate);
· The WFR metric restricted to $\mathcal{M}$ is well-defined and positive-definite (this is a hypothesis on $\mathcal{M}$ — e.g., the Gaussian family, where explicit formulas are available).

On $\mathcal{M}$, define the hybrid metric
g^{\text{hyb}}_{ij}(\theta) = (1-\lambda) g^{\text{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta) + \lambda g^{\text{WFR}}_{ij}(\theta), \qquad \lambda \in [0,1]. \tag{7.1}

Proposition 7.2 (Positive-definiteness). If $g^{\text{WFR}}$ is positive-definite on $\mathcal{M}$ and $\lambda > 0$, then $g^{\text{hyb}}$ is positive-definite on $\mathcal{M}$, even where $g^{\text{Fisher}}$ degenerates.

Proof. For any non-zero tangent vector $v \in T_\theta \mathcal{M}$,
v^\top g^{\text{hyb}} v = (1-\lambda) v^\top g^{\text{Fisher}} v + \lambda v^\top g^{\text{WFR}} v \ge \lambda v^\top g^{\text{WFR}} v > 0. \qquad \square

Remark 7.3. The WFR metric on a parameterized family is defined via the Benamou–Brenier formulation for unbalanced transport restricted to $\mathcal{M}$. This requires $\mathcal{M}$ to be a submanifold on which the WFR metric is well-defined; we state this as a standing hypothesis on $\mathcal{M}$.

7.2 Natural Gradient Flow with Hybrid Metric

The free energy gradient flow becomes
\dot{\theta} = -(g^{\text{hyb}})^{-1} \nabla_\theta \mathcal{F}(\theta; o). \tag{7.2}

Proposition 7.4 (Well-posedness). If $g^{\text{Fisher}}$ degenerates on a set of measure zero in $\Theta_{\mathcal{M}}$ and $g^{\text{WFR}}$ is positive-definite, then (7.2) has a unique local solution for every initial condition $\theta_0 \in \Theta_{\mathcal{M}}$.

Proof. By Proposition 7.2, $g^{\text{hyb}}$ is positive-definite. The right-hand side of (7.2) is locally Lipschitz under regularity of $\mathcal{F}$. Apply Picard–Lindelöf. $\square$

7.3 Thermodynamic Length

The thermodynamic length of a path $\gamma: [0,1] \to \mathcal{M}$ is
\mathcal{L}_{\text{th}}[\gamma] = \int_0^1 \sqrt{g^{\text{hyb}}_{ij}(\gamma(t)) \dot{\gamma}^i(t) \dot{\gamma}^j(t)}\,dt. \tag{7.3}


This defines the cognitive load of a belief update.

