9. Mean-Field Games with Adversarial Agents

9.1 Microscopic Model with Adversarial Fraction

Consider $N$ agents, of which a fraction $\rho$ are adversarial (Byzantine). Cooperative agents solve
dX_t^i = a_t^i\,dt + \sqrt{2\nu}\,dW_t^i, \qquad J^i(a^i) = \mathbb{E}\!\left[\int_0^T \left( \frac12 \|a_t^i\|^2 + \mathcal{F}(\theta_t^i; o_t) + V(X_t^i, m_t^N) \right) dt \right].

9.2 Reputation-Aware Mean-Field Game

Following the FedMFG framework, we introduce a reputation variable $r^i \in [0,1]$ updated by cosine similarity between the agent's action and the mean-field action:
r^i_{t+1} = r^i_t + \kappa \left( \cos(a^i_t, \bar{a}_t) - r^i_t \right), \tag{9.1}


where $\bar{a}_t$ is the mean-field action. Agents with reputation below threshold $\theta_r$ are excluded from the field update.

Remark 9.1. The cosine similarity $\cos(a^i, \bar{a})$ requires the action space $\mathcal{A}$ to be a real inner product space. We assume $\mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p$ with the Euclidean inner product.

Theorem 9.2 (Existence of mean-field equilibrium). Under the FedMFG framework, the reputation-aware mean-field game admits a unique Nash equilibrium in the space of Lipschitz controls.

Theorem 9.3 (Byzantine robustness, conditional on FedMFG citation status). Let $\lambda_{\min}(D)$ denote the smallest eigenvalue of the diffusion tensor and $\kappa$ the reputation rate. Under the assumptions of Theorem 9.2, if
\rho < \rho_c = \frac{1}{2} \left( 1 - \frac{\kappa}{\kappa + \lambda_{\min}(D)} \right), \tag{9.2}


then the alignment divergence at the NESS satisfies
\mathcal{A}(\Psi^*, \mathcal{V}) \le \epsilon + C \rho


for a constant $C$ depending on the reputation threshold $\theta_r$ and the spectral gap of the linearized operator.

Proof sketch. The reputation mechanism induces a contraction on the space of admissible controls for cooperative agents: adversarial agents with reputation below $\theta_r$ are excluded. The perturbation of the Fokker–Planck equation by the remaining adversarial agents is bounded by $\rho$. A spectral-gap estimate for the linearized FP operator, combined with the alignment projection (Proposition 3.6, now unconditional), yields the bound. $\square$

Remark 9.4. The critical fraction (9.2) is stated conditionally on the FedMFG framework. The derivation requires the spectral analysis of the linearized coupled FP–HJB system.

Remark 9.5 (Citation status). The FedMFG framework is cited here as a modeling template. The exact statement and proof of Theorems 9.2–9.3 require verification against the primary source; the constants in (9.2) depend on the specific reputation update and may differ from the form given.

9.3 Effect of the Diffusion Floor

The floor $D \ge D_{\min}$ in (4.1) ensures $\lambda_{\min}(D) \ge \lambda_{\min}(D_{\min}) > 0$, so $\rho_c$ in (9.2) remains bounded below by a positive constant even when local entropy production is high. Without the floor, $\rho_c \to 0$ as $\sigma \to \sigma_{\max}$, degrading robustness.

