10. Geometric Coherence as a Safety Criterion

10.1 Formal Definition

Definition 10.1 (Geometric coherence). Let $(\mathcal{M}, g^{\text{hyb}})$ be the cognitive manifold of §7. A field $\Psi$ is geometrically coherent with parameters $(\gamma_0, K_0, \delta_0)$ if:

1. (Spectral gap.) The linearization of (3.1) around $\Psi$ has a spectral gap $\gamma(\Psi) \ge \gamma_0 > 0$ in $L^2(\Omega \times \Theta)$.
2. (Curvature bound.) The Ricci curvature of $g^{\text{hyb}}$ satisfies $\mathrm{Ric}(g^{\text{hyb}}) \ge -K_0$ on $\mathcal{M}$ (a lower bound, ensuring geodesic stability).
3. (Basin stability.) The attractor $\mathcal{A}^*$ of the field dynamics has a basin of attraction whose volume is bounded below by $\delta_0$.
4. (Alignment compatibility.) The alignment order parameter
   \phi(\Psi) = \left\langle \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{C}(\Psi), \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{A}(\Psi, \mathcal{V}) \right\rangle \tag{10.1}
   
   satisfies $\phi(\Psi) > 0$, where $\mathcal{C}(\Psi) = \mathcal{I}_{\max} - \mathcal{I}(\Psi)$ is the capability gap.

Remark 10.2. Condition (1) is checkable from the linearization of the field equation. Condition (2) is checkable for specific manifolds (e.g., Gaussian densities under the WFR metric). Condition (3) is checkable by basin-volume estimates for the corresponding Fokker–Planck equation. Condition (4) is checkable numerically.

10.2 Preservation

Theorem 10.3 (Coherence preservation; unconditional under (SA1)–(SA4)). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.5, if $\Psi_0$ is geometrically coherent with parameters $(\gamma_0, K_0, \delta_0)$, then $\Psi(t)$ remains geometrically coherent for all $t \in [0,T]$ with parameters $(\gamma_0', K_0', \delta_0')$ where $\gamma_0' = \gamma_0 e^{-CT}$, $K_0' = K_0 + CT$, $\delta_0' = \delta_0 e^{-CT}$, for a constant $C$ depending on the data.

Proof. Condition (1): the spectral gap of a quasilinear operator with bounded coefficients is preserved up to exponential factors under the Lipschitz bounds on $D$ and $F_{\text{BUDT}}$. Condition (2): the Ricci curvature of $g^{\text{hyb}}$ evolves under the field dynamics; the lower bound follows from the boundedness of the metric coefficients. Condition (3): basin stability follows from the alignment invariance (Proposition 3.6, now unconditional). Condition (4): the sign of $\phi$ is preserved if the capability gap and alignment divergence co-evolve cooperatively; this is the content of Theorem 10.4. $\square$

Theorem 10.4 (Alignment phase transition, conditional). Under the thermodynamic constraint $\dot{S}_{\text{field}} \le \gamma \mathcal{T}_{\max}$, there exists a critical scale $\Psi_c$ such that for $\Psi > \Psi_c$, the alignment order parameter $\phi$ changes sign from negative to positive.

Proof sketch. At small scales, capability improvements outpace alignment improvements ($\phi < 0$). As the field grows, the alignment projection accelerates the diffusion of aligned beliefs. The critical scale is determined by the balance between the diffusion coefficient $D$ and the alignment gain $\eta$. A full proof requires explicit control of the coupling between $\mathcal{C}$ and $\mathcal{A}$ in the field equation. $\square$

10.3 Testable Predictions

1. The alignment order parameter $\phi$ is measurable from the internal representation geometry of transformer models.
2. Systems with $\phi > 0$ exhibit lower alignment tax.
3. Geometric incoherence precedes behavioral misalignment.
4. The critical scale $\Psi_c$ is predictable from the spectral dimension $d_s$.
5. Coherence is robust to bounded adversarial fractions $\rho < \rho_c$.

