11.1 Fractional Coupling

To be consistent with the Camilli–De Maio framework for time-fractional mean-field games, all three equations carry the same Caputo derivative:
\begin{aligned} {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi &= \nabla \cdot (D(\sigma) \nabla \Psi) + \mathcal{G}(\Psi, m) + \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi), \\ {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha m &= \nu \Delta_x m + \kappa \Delta_{\text{hyb}} m + \nabla_x \cdot (m \nabla_x u), \\ {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha u &= -\nu \Delta_x u - \kappa \Delta_{\text{hyb}} u + \frac12 \|\nabla_x u\|^2 - \mathcal{F}(\Psi, m), \end{aligned} \tag{11.1}


with Neumann boundary conditions, initial conditions $(\Psi_0, m_0)$, and terminal condition $u(T) = 0$ interpreted in the sense of the backward fractional problem (see Remark 2.7 and Camilli–De Maio). Here $\Delta_{\text{hyb}}$ is the Laplace–Beltrami operator of the hybrid metric $g^{\text{hyb}}$.

Remark 11.1. The previous revision mixed integer and fractional time derivatives. This is inconsistent with the time-fractional MFG theory. The system (11.1) uses the Caputo derivative throughout.

11.2 Existence

Theorem 11.2 (Global existence of the coupled system; unconditional under (SA1)–(SA4) and Camilli–De Maio hypotheses). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.5, the Lipschitz and monotonicity assumptions of Camilli–De Maio on the HJB and FP equations, and the alignment-compatibility condition (3.6), the coupled system (11.1) admits a mild solution
\Psi \in C([0,T]; H^1), \quad m \in L^2(0,T; \mathcal{P}(\Omega \times \Theta)), \quad u \in C^{1,2}([0,T] \times \Omega \times \Theta).

Proof sketch. Construct a fixed-point operator on the product space
\mathcal{X} = C([0,T]; H^1) \times L^2(0,T; \mathcal{P}(\Omega \times \Theta)) \times C^{1,2}([0,T] \times \Omega \times \Theta)


by solving the three equations sequentially. The fractional field equation has a solution by Theorem 4.5. The time-fractional HJB and FP equations have solutions by Camilli–De Maio. The alignment projection preserves the invariant convex set unconditionally (Proposition 3.6). Apply the Schauder–Tychonoff fixed-point theorem. $\square$

11.3 Convergence to Alignment-Stable NESS

Theorem 11.3 (Convergence, conditional). Under the Lyapunov functional
\mathcal{E}(t) = \frac12 \|\Psi\|_{L^2}^2 + \frac12 \|\nabla \Psi\|_{L^2}^2 + \lambda \mathcal{A}(\Psi, \mathcal{V}), \tag{11.2}


the time-dependent solution $(\Psi(t), m(t))$ converges strongly in $H^1 \times L^1$ to a non-equilibrium steady state $(\Psi^*, m^*)$ with $\mathcal{A}(\Psi^*, \mathcal{V}) \le \epsilon$.

Proof sketch. The fractional Barbălat lemma applied to $\mathcal{E}(t)$ gives $\mathcal{E}(t) \to \mathcal{E}^*$ and ${}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{E}(t) \to 0$, forcing convergence to a stationary solution. The alignment constraint is preserved by Proposition 3.6 unconditionally. A complete proof requires the fractional Barbălat lemma in the form applicable to Caputo derivatives; we cite the relevant literature. $\square$

