Appendix A: The Fractional Lyapunov Inequality for Convex Functionals

This appendix provides the full statement, proof strategy, and applicability verification for the fractional Lyapunov inequality that replaces the invalid fractional chain rule and the previously conjectural sign condition on the fractional Leibniz remainder.

A.1 Finite-Dimensional Statement and Proof

Theorem A.1 (Gomoyunov, 2017, Theorem 1). Let $\alpha \in (0,1)$, $T > 0$, and let $V: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ be convex and continuously differentiable. Let $x: [0,T] \to \mathbb{R}^n$ be absolutely continuous with ${}^C_0 D_t^\alpha x \in L^1(0,T; \mathbb{R}^n)$. Then for almost every $t \in (0,T)$,
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha V(x(t)) \le \left\langle \nabla V(x(t)), \, {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha x(t) \right\rangle. \tag{A.1}

Proof. We use the alternative representation of the Caputo derivative valid for $f \in AC([0,T])$ with $f(0) = 0$:
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha f(t) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \frac{f(t)}{t^\alpha} + \frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \int_0^t \frac{f(t) - f(s)}{(t-s)^{1+\alpha}} \, ds. \tag{A.2}

For general $f$ with $f(0) \neq 0$, apply (A.2) to $g(t) = f(t) - f(0)$; the constant $f(0)$ has zero Caputo derivative, so (A.2) holds with $f(t) - f(0)$ in the first term:
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha f(t) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \frac{f(t) - f(0)}{t^\alpha} + \frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \int_0^t \frac{f(t) - f(s)}{(t-s)^{1+\alpha}} \, ds. \tag{A.3}

Apply (A.3) to $f(t) = V(x(t))$:
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha V(x(t)) = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \frac{V(x(t)) - V(x(0))}{t^\alpha} + \frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \int_0^t \frac{V(x(t)) - V(x(s))}{(t-s)^{1+\alpha}} \, ds. \tag{A.4}

Apply (A.3) to each component of $x$ and take the inner product with $\nabla V(x(t))$:
\left\langle \nabla V(x(t)), \, {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha x(t) \right\rangle = \frac{1}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \frac{\langle \nabla V(x(t)), x(t) - x(0) \rangle}{t^\alpha} + \frac{\alpha}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)} \int_0^t \frac{\langle \nabla V(x(t)), x(t) - x(s) \rangle}{(t-s)^{1+\alpha}} \, ds. \tag{A.5}

By convexity of $V$, for every $s \in [0,t]$,
V(x(t)) - V(x(s)) \le \langle \nabla V(x(t)), x(t) - x(s) \rangle. \tag{A.6}

Substituting (A.6) into (A.4) and comparing term-by-term with (A.5) yields (A.1). $\square$

A.2 Hilbert-Space Extension

Theorem A.2 (Hilbert-space fractional Lyapunov inequality). Let $\mathcal{F}: H^1(\Omega \times \Theta) \to \mathbb{R}$ be convex and Fréchet differentiable with gradient Lipschitz in $L^2(\Omega \times \Theta)$. Let $\Psi \in C([0,T]; H^1) \cap C^1((0,T]; L^2)$ with ${}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi \in L^1(0,T; L^2)$. Then for almost every $t \in (0,T)$,
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{F}(\Psi(t)) \le \left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{F}}{\delta \Psi}(\Psi(t)), \, {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi(t) \right\rangle. \tag{A.7}

Proof strategy. Let $\{e_k\}_{k \ge 1} \subset H^1$ be an orthonormal basis of $L^2$. Define the Galerkin projections $\Psi_N(t) = \sum_{k=1}^N \langle \Psi(t), e_k \rangle e_k$ and $\mathcal{F}_N = \mathcal{F}|_{V_N}$ where $V_N = \mathrm{span}\{e_1, \ldots, e_N\}$. Then $\mathcal{F}_N: V_N \cong \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}$ is convex and $C^1$, and $\Psi_N$ satisfies the regularity hypotheses of Theorem A.1. Applying (A.1) to each $(\mathcal{F}_N, \Psi_N)$ yields
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{F}_N(\Psi_N(t)) \le \left\langle \nabla \mathcal{F}_N(\Psi_N(t)), \, {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi_N(t) \right\rangle. \tag{A.8}

Under the stated regularity, $\Psi_N \to \Psi$ in $C([0,T]; H^1) \cap C^1((0,T]; L^2)$ and $\mathcal{F}_N(\Psi_N) \to \mathcal{F}(\Psi)$ in $C([0,T]; \mathbb{R})$. The Caputo derivative is continuous with respect to these topologies (as a bounded operator from $C^1((0,T]; L^2) \cap L^1(0,T;L^2)$ to $L^1(0,T;L^2)$), and the right-hand side of (A.8) converges to the right-hand side of (A.7) in $L^1(0,T)$. Passing to the limit yields (A.7). $\square$

A.3 Applicability to the Alignment Functional

Lemma A.3 (Convexity and gradient Lipschitzness of $\mathcal{A}$). Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the regularized alignment functional of Definition 3.1 with $\Psi \ge \delta_{\min} > 0$ and $\mathcal{V} \ge \nu_{\min} > 0$. Then:

(i) $\mathcal{A}$ is convex in $\Psi$ on $H^1$.
(ii) $\mathcal{A}$ is Fréchet differentiable with gradient given by Lemma 3.4.
(iii) The gradient map $\Psi \mapsto \delta \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi$ is Lipschitz on bounded subsets of $H^1 \cap \{\Psi \ge \delta_{\min}\}$.

Proof. (i) The map $\Psi \mapsto p_{x,t} = \Psi/\rho$ is affine in $\Psi$ (as a map into the space of signed measures with total mass 1). The map $p \mapsto D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p \| \mathcal{V})$ is convex on the simplex. Composition of an affine map with a convex function is convex. Integration against the non-negative weight $w$ preserves convexity.

(ii) Follows from Lemma 3.4.

(iii) The functional derivative (3.4) contains $\log p$, which is Lipschitz on $\{p \ge \delta_{\min}/\|\rho\|_\infty\}$, and the normalization $1/\rho$, which is Lipschitz on $\{\rho \ge \delta_{\min}\}$. Under (SA1) and the maximum principle for (3.1), these lower bounds persist on bounded time intervals. $\square$

Corollary A.4 (Applicability of the fractional Lyapunov inequality to $\mathcal{A}$). Under (SA1)–(SA2), the alignment functional $\mathcal{A}$ satisfies the hypotheses of Theorem A.2. Consequently,
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{A}(\Psi(t), \mathcal{V}) \le \left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}(\Psi(t)), \, {}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \Psi(t) \right\rangle \quad \text{a.e. on } (0,T). \tag{A.9}

Remark A.5. Corollary A.4 is the precise statement that replaces the invalid fractional chain rule and Conjecture 3.10 of the prior revision. No separate analysis of a remainder term $R(t)$ is required: the inequality (A.9) is intrinsic to convex Lyapunov functionals under Caputo dynamics.

