Appendix B: Functional Derivative of the Alignment Functional

This appendix provides the corrected computation of $\delta \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi$, addressing the error in the prior revision (an extraneous $+1$ inside the bracket).

B.1 Setup

Let $\Psi \in H^1(\Omega \times \Theta)$ with $\Psi \ge \delta_{\min} > 0$, and define
\rho(x) = \int_\Theta \Psi(x,\theta) \, d\mu_\Theta(\theta), \qquad p_x(\theta) = \frac{\Psi(x,\theta)}{\rho(x)}. \tag{B.1}

The alignment functional is
\mathcal{A}(\Psi, \mathcal{V}) = \int_\Omega w(x) \, D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_x \| \mathcal{V}) \, dx, \qquad D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p \| \mathcal{V}) = \int_\Theta p(\theta) \log\frac{p(\theta)}{\mathcal{V}(\theta)} \, d\mu_\Theta(\theta). \tag{B.2}

B.2 First Variation

Let $\delta \Psi \in H^1$ be a variation. Then
\delta \rho = \int_\Theta \delta \Psi \, d\mu_\Theta, \qquad \delta p = \frac{\delta \Psi - p \, \delta \rho}{\rho}. \tag{B.3}

The first variation of $D_{\mathrm{KL}}$ is
\delta D_{\mathrm{KL}} = \int_\Theta \delta p \left( \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} + 1 \right) d\mu_\Theta. \tag{B.4}

Substituting (B.3):
\delta D_{\mathrm{KL}} = \frac{1}{\rho} \int_\Theta \delta \Psi \left( \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} + 1 \right) d\mu_\Theta - \frac{\delta \rho}{\rho} \int_\Theta p \left( \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} + 1 \right) d\mu_\Theta. \tag{B.5}

Using $\int_\Theta p \, d\mu_\Theta = 1$ and $\int_\Theta p \log(p/\mathcal{V}) \, d\mu_\Theta = D_{\mathrm{KL}}$:
\int_\Theta p \left( \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} + 1 \right) d\mu_\Theta = D_{\mathrm{KL}} + 1. \tag{B.6}

Therefore
\delta D_{\mathrm{KL}} = \frac{1}{\rho} \int_\Theta \delta \Psi \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} \, d\mu_\Theta + \frac{\delta \rho}{\rho} - \frac{\delta \rho}{\rho}(D_{\mathrm{KL}} + 1) = \frac{1}{\rho} \int_\Theta \delta \Psi \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} \, d\mu_\Theta - \frac{\delta \rho}{\rho} D_{\mathrm{KL}}. \tag{B.7}

The two $\delta \rho/\rho$ terms cancel. Substituting $\delta \rho = \int_\Theta \delta \Psi \, d\mu_\Theta$:
\delta D_{\mathrm{KL}} = \int_\Theta \frac{\delta \Psi}{\rho} \left[ \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} - D_{\mathrm{KL}} \right] d\mu_\Theta. \tag{B.8}

B.3 Functional Derivative

Multiplying by $w(x)$ and integrating over $\Omega$:
\delta \mathcal{A} = \int_\Omega \int_\Theta \frac{w(x)}{\rho(x)} \left[ \log\frac{p_x(\theta)}{\mathcal{V}(\theta)} - D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_x \| \mathcal{V}) \right] \delta \Psi(x,\theta) \, d\mu_\Theta(\theta) \, dx. \tag{B.9}

Hence, by definition of the $L^2$ functional derivative,
\boxed{\frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}(x,\theta) = \frac{w(x)}{\rho(x)} \left[ \log\frac{p_x(\theta)}{\mathcal{V}(\theta)} - D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_x \| \mathcal{V}) \right].} \tag{B.10}

B.4 Consistency Checks

Check 1 (Vanishing at the reference). If $p_x = \mathcal{V}$ for all $x$, then $D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_x \| \mathcal{V}) = 0$ and $\log(p_x/\mathcal{V}) = 0$, so $\delta \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi = 0$ identically. This is the correct behavior: at the global minimum of $\mathcal{A}$, the functional derivative vanishes.

Check 2 (Sign of the prior revision's error). The prior revision displayed
\frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi} = w \left[ \log\frac{p}{\mathcal{V}} + 1 - D_{\mathrm{KL}} \right] \frac{1}{\rho}. \tag{B.11}


The $+1$ term is spurious: it cancels against the normalization contribution from $\rho$, as shown in (B.7). With the $+1$ retained, the derivative would not vanish at $p = \mathcal{V}$, which is inconsistent with $\mathcal{A}$ attaining its minimum there.

