Appendix C: Dissipativity Estimates for the Diffusion and Projection Terms

This appendix provides the corrected version of Lemma 3.7 (diffusion dissipation) and the detailed computation of the projection term's contribution.

C.1 Diffusion Term

Lemma C.1 (Diffusion dissipation; corrected). Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the regularized alignment functional (3.2) with $\Psi \ge \delta_{\min} > 0$. Then for any bounded measurable $D \ge D_{\min} > 0$,
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, \, \nabla \cdot (D \nabla \Psi) \right\rangle = -\int_{\Omega \times \Theta} D \, \left\langle \frac{\delta^2 \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi^2} \nabla \Psi, \, \nabla \Psi \right\rangle d(\mathbf{x},\theta) \le 0. \tag{C.1}

Proof. Integration by parts using Neumann boundary conditions:
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, \nabla \cdot (D \nabla \Psi) \right\rangle = -\int_{\Omega \times \Theta} D \, \nabla \Psi \cdot \nabla\!\left( \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi} \right) d(\mathbf{x},\theta). \tag{C.2}

By the chain rule for the functional derivative,
\nabla\!\left( \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi} \right) = \frac{\delta^2 \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi^2} \nabla \Psi. \tag{C.3}

Substituting (C.3) into (C.2) and using the positive semi-definiteness of $\delta^2 \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi^2$ (convexity of $\mathcal{A}$):
-\int D \, \left\langle \frac{\delta^2 \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi^2} \nabla \Psi, \nabla \Psi \right\rangle d(\mathbf{x},\theta) \le 0. \qquad \square \tag{C.4}

Remark C.2. The prior revision wrote the diffusion contribution as $-\int D |\nabla \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{A}|^2 \le 0$. That specific quadratic form in $\nabla \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{A}$ is not equivalent to (C.1) and is not generally valid. The correct statement is the non-positivity of (C.1), not the stronger (and false) bound in the prior revision.

C.2 Projection Term

Lemma C.3 (Projection dissipation). Let $\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) = -\eta \nabla_\Psi \sigma_k(\mathcal{A}(\Psi,\mathcal{V}) - \epsilon)$ with $\sigma_k$ the softplus function. Then
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, \, \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) \right\rangle = -\eta \, \sigma_k'(\mathcal{A} - \epsilon) \, \left\| \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi} \right\|_{L^2}^2 \le 0. \tag{C.5}

Moreover, on $\{\mathcal{A} > \epsilon\}$, there exists $c_0 > 0$ (depending on $k$ and $\epsilon$) such that
\left\langle \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}, \, \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) \right\rangle \le -c_0 \left\| \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi} \right\|_{L^2}^2. \tag{C.6}

Proof. By the chain rule,
\Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}(\Psi) = -\eta \, \sigma_k'(\mathcal{A} - \epsilon) \frac{\delta \mathcal{A}}{\delta \Psi}. \tag{C.7}

Taking the inner product with $\delta \mathcal{A}/\delta \Psi$ and using $\sigma_k' \ge 0$ yields (C.5). On $\{\mathcal{A} > \epsilon\}$, the softplus derivative satisfies $\sigma_k'(\mathcal{A} - \epsilon) \ge \sigma_k'(0) = 1/2$ for all $k > 0$. Hence (C.6) holds with $c_0 = \eta/2$. $\square$

C.3 Combined Differential Inequality

Combining Corollary A.4, Lemma C.1, Lemma C.3, and the alignment-compatibility condition (3.6),
{}^C_0 D_t^\alpha \mathcal{A}(\Psi(t), \mathcal{V}) \le -c \, (\mathcal{A}(\Psi(t), \mathcal{V}) - \epsilon)_+ \quad \text{a.e. on } \{t : \mathcal{A} > \epsilon\}, \tag{C.8}


for a constant $c > 0$ depending on $\eta$, $\epsilon$, and the boundedness constants of $F_{\text{BUDT}}$. This is the differential inequality required by Corollary 2.2.

