Appendix F: Well-Definedness of the Hybrid Fisher–WFR Metric

This appendix clarifies the hypotheses under which the hybrid metric $g^{\text{hyb}} = (1-\lambda) g^{\text{Fisher}} + \lambda g^{\text{WFR}}$ is well-defined and positive-definite.

F.1 The Fisher–Rao Metric

Let $\mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{P}(\Theta)$ be a parameterized family $\{p_\theta\}_{\theta \in \Theta_{\mathcal{M}}}$ with $\Theta_{\mathcal{M}} \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ open. The Fisher information matrix is
g^{\text{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta) = \int_\Theta \partial_i \log p_\theta \, \partial_j \log p_\theta \, p_\theta \, d\mu_\Theta(\theta). \tag{F.1}

It is symmetric and positive semi-definite, possibly degenerate.

F.2 The Wasserstein–Fisher–Rao Metric

The WFR metric on $\mathcal{P}(\Theta)$ is defined via the Benamou–Brenier formulation for unbalanced transport:
g^{\text{WFR}}(\dot{\theta}, \dot{\theta}) = \inf_{(\mu, v)} \left\{ \int_0^1 \int_\Theta \left( |v_t|^2 + |\nabla \phi_t|^2 \right) d\mu_t \, dt \right\}, \tag{F.2}


subject to the continuity equation $\partial_t \mu_t + \nabla \cdot (\mu_t v_t) = \mu_t \nabla \phi_t$ with $\mu_0 = \mu_1 = p_\theta$, where the infimum is over paths in $\mathcal{M}$ tangent to $\dot{\theta}$ at $\theta$.

F.3 Well-Definedness Hypothesis

Hypothesis (H-WFR). The family $\mathcal{M}$ is such that:

(i) The WFR metric restricted to $\mathcal{M}$ is well-defined and finite for all tangent vectors in $T_\theta \mathcal{M}$.
(ii) The WFR metric is positive-definite on $\mathcal{M}$.
(iii) The WFR metric depends smoothly on $\theta \in \Theta_{\mathcal{M}}$.

Remark F.1. Hypothesis (H-WFR) holds for the Gaussian family $\mathcal{M} = \{N(m, \Sigma) : m \in \mathbb{R}^d, \Sigma \in \mathrm{Sym}^+_d\}$, where explicit formulas for both $g^{\text{Fisher}}$ and $g^{\text{WFR}}$ are available (e.g., in the WFR gradient flow literature). It may fail for degenerate families.

F.4 Positive-Definiteness

Proposition F.2. Under (H-WFR) and $\lambda \in (0,1]$, the hybrid metric
g^{\text{hyb}}_{ij}(\theta) = (1-\lambda) g^{\text{Fisher}}_{ij}(\theta) + \lambda g^{\text{WFR}}_{ij}(\theta) \tag{F.3}


is positive-definite on $\mathcal{M}$, even where $g^{\text{Fisher}}$ degenerates.

Proof. For any non-zero $v \in T_\theta \mathcal{M}$,
v^\top g^{\text{hyb}} v = (1-\lambda) v^\top g^{\text{Fisher}} v + \lambda v^\top g^{\text{WFR}} v \ge \lambda v^\top g^{\text{WFR}} v > 0, \tag{F.4}


using the positive-definiteness of $g^{\text{WFR}}$ (H-WFR (ii)). $\square$

Remark F.3. The prior revision wrote $g^{\text{hyb}} = g^{\text{Fisher}} + g^{\text{WFR}}$ without placing them on a common manifold. Since $g^{\text{Fisher}}$ acts on parameter space and $g^{\text{WFR}}$ acts on the space of measures, the sum is not well-defined without the common-manifold construction. Construction 7.1 (Hypothesis (H-WFR) above) resolves this.

