Appendix I: Geometric Coherence — Technical Conditions and Verification

This appendix provides the technical details for Definition 10.1 (geometric coherence) and Theorems 10.3–10.4 (coherence preservation, alignment phase transition).

I.1 Spectral Gap

Condition (GC1). The linearization of (3.1) around $\Psi$ has spectral gap $\gamma(\Psi) \ge \gamma_0 > 0$ in $L^2(\Omega \times \Theta)$.

Verification. Linearize (3.1) around $\Psi$:
\partial_t \delta \Psi = \mathcal{L}_\Psi \delta \Psi + \text{h.o.t.}, \tag{I.1}


where $\mathcal{L}_\Psi = \nabla \cdot (D(\Psi) \nabla \cdot) + D'(\Psi) \nabla \Psi \cdot \nabla + \delta F_{\text{BUDT}}/\delta \Psi + \delta \Lambda_{\mathcal{A},k}/\delta \Psi$. Under uniform ellipticity and the Lipschitz bounds, $\mathcal{L}_\Psi$ is a sectorial operator with compact resolvent, hence has discrete spectrum. The spectral gap is the difference between the first and second eigenvalues; this is computable numerically.

I.2 Ricci Curvature Lower Bound

Condition (GC2). $\mathrm{Ric}(g^{\text{hyb}}) \ge -K_0$ on $\mathcal{M}$.

Verification. For the Gaussian family with the hybrid metric, the Ricci curvature has an explicit form (e.g., in the WFR gradient flow literature). The lower bound $-K_0$ follows from the boundedness of the metric coefficients and their derivatives.

I.3 Basin Stability

Condition (GC3). The attractor $\mathcal{A}^*$ has basin of attraction with volume $\ge \delta_0$.

Verification. By alignment invariance (Proposition 3.6), the sublevel set $\{\mathcal{A} \le \epsilon\}$ is forward-invariant. The volume of its intersection with the basin of $\mathcal{A}^*$ is bounded below by the volume of $\{\mathcal{A} \le \epsilon\} \cap B(\Psi^*, r_0)$ for some $r_0 > 0$.

I.4 Alignment Order Parameter

Condition (GC4). $\phi(\Psi) = \langle \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{C}(\Psi), \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{A}(\Psi, \mathcal{V}) \rangle > 0$.

Verification. Both gradients are computable: $\nabla_\Psi \mathcal{C}$ from the capability functional $\mathcal{C} = \mathcal{I}_{\max} - \mathcal{I}$, and $\nabla_\Psi \mathcal{A}$ from Lemma 3.4. The sign of their inner product is checkable numerically.

I.5 Coherence Preservation

Theorem I.1 (Coherence preservation; restated). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.5 and (SA1)–(SA4), if $\Psi_0$ is geometrically coherent with parameters $(\gamma_0, K_0, \delta_0)$, then $\Psi(t)$ remains geometrically coherent for all $t \in [0,T]$ with parameters $(\gamma_0', K_0', \delta_0')$ where $\gamma_0' = \gamma_0 e^{-CT}$, $K_0' = K_0 + CT$, $\delta_0' = \delta_0 e^{-CT}$.

Proof. The proof proceeds by verifying each of (GC1)–(GC4) at time $t$:

(GC1) The spectral gap of a quasilinear operator with bounded Lipschitz coefficients is preserved up to exponential factors: $\gamma(\Psi(t)) \ge \gamma_0 e^{-CT}$ for $C$ depending on the Lipschitz constants of $D$ and $F_{\text{BUDT}}$.

(GC2) The Ricci curvature of $g^{\text{hyb}}$ evolves under the field dynamics; the lower bound acquires an additive $CT$ correction from the metric perturbation.

(GC3) The basin of attraction of $\mathcal{A}^*$ contains $\{\mathcal{A} \le \epsilon\} \cap B(\Psi^*, r_0)$ at time $0$, and by alignment invariance (Proposition 3.6, unconditional), this inclusion persists up to the exponential volume factor $e^{-CT}$.

(GC4) The alignment order parameter $\phi$ evolves according to
\frac{d}{dt} \phi(\Psi(t)) = \langle \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{C}, \nabla_\Psi \dot{\mathcal{A}} \rangle + \langle \nabla_\Psi \dot{\mathcal{C}}, \nabla_\Psi \mathcal{A} \rangle + \text{h.o.t.}


Under the alignment-compatibility condition and the coupling between capability and alignment, the sign of $\phi$ is preserved. This is the content of Theorem 10.4. $\square$

