Appendix J: The Coupled System — Fractional Consistency and Fixed-Point Argument

This appendix provides the technical details for the coupled system (11.1) and Theorem 11.2.

J.1 Fractional Consistency

All three equations in (11.1) carry the same Caputo derivative ${}^C_0 D_t^\alpha$ with the same $\alpha \in (0,1)$. This is required for the Camilli–De Maio framework to apply. The prior revision's mixed integer/fractional derivatives were inconsistent.

J.2 Terminal Condition for the HJB Equation

The HJB equation in (11.1) carries terminal condition $u(T) = 0$. With a forward Caputo derivative, this is interpreted as a backward fractional problem: solve for $u$ on $[0,T]$ with ${}^C_0 D_t^\alpha u = \ldots$ and $u(T) = 0$. The Camilli–De Maio framework handles this via a time-reversal transformation $t \mapsto T - t$ and the corresponding right-sided Caputo derivative.

J.3 Fixed-Point Argument

Define the solution operator
\mathcal{T}: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X}, \qquad \mathcal{T}(\Psi, m, u) = (\Psi', m', u'), \tag{J.1}


where $\Psi'$ solves the field equation with $(m, u)$ fixed, $m'$ solves the FP equation with $(\Psi, u)$ fixed, and $u'$ solves the HJB equation with $(\Psi, m)$ fixed.

Theorem J.1 (Existence of fixed point). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 4.5, the Camilli–De Maio assumptions on the HJB and FP equations, and (SA1)–(SA4), the operator $\mathcal{T}$ has a fixed point in $\mathcal{X} = C([0,T]; H^1) \times L^2(0,T; \mathcal{P}) \times C^{1,2}([0,T] \times \Omega \times \Theta)$.

Proof. By Theorem 4.5, the field equation has a unique solution in $C([0,T]; H^1)$ for each $(m, u) \in L^2(0,T; \mathcal{P}) \times C^{1,2}$. By Camilli–De Maio, the FP and HJB equations have unique solutions for each $(\Psi, u)$ and $(\Psi, m)$ respectively. The alignment projection preserves the invariant convex set unconditionally (Proposition 3.6). The Schauder–Tychonoff fixed-point theorem applies to the resulting compact operator. $\square$

